WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Chapter one showed that the energy hill sets which way a solute moves.

2
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.320
So how can data reveal whether it crossed through a channel or a carrier?

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.840
Watch the traffic, not the protein's name.

4
00:00:16.283 --> 00:00:20.123
An open channel is a continuous pore between both sides.

5
00:00:21.543 --> 00:00:24.463
Ions stream through without waiting for a transport cycle.

6
00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:30.363
A channel can switch between open and closed; a carrier must finish each

7
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:31.803
alternating-access cycle.

8
00:00:32.923 --> 00:00:37.983
A carrier opens to one side, closes around its solute, then opens to the other.

9
00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:40.983
That shape change limits each trip.

10
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:45.517
A single channel gives the detector square current steps.

11
00:00:46.797 --> 00:00:51.337
Millions of ions per second can cross one pore, producing measurable current.

12
00:00:52.657 --> 00:00:54.057
Each jump marks an opening.

13
00:00:55.277 --> 00:00:58.717
The step height is the unitary current through one open channel.

14
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:03.957
The fraction of recording time spent open is its open probability.

15
00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:06.757
Those are different measurements.

16
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.740
Now a drug makes the openings last longer, but every step keeps the same height.

17
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.920
What changed: current through one open channel, or open probability?

18
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:20.500
Decide from the trace.

19
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:30.953
Open probability increased: the channel spent more of its time open.

20
00:01:32.313 --> 00:01:36.413
Unchanged step height means unchanged unitary current at this voltage.

21
00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:42.433
A whole-cell current alone mixes channel number, unitary current, and open

22
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:44.953
probability, so it cannot separate them.

23
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:47.873
Carriers leave a different signature.

24
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:53.953
Raise substrate concentration and their limited binding sites become occupied.

25
00:01:55.273 --> 00:01:57.513
The rate bends toward a ceiling called Vmax.

26
00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:03.753
A pore or simple diffusion can stay nearly proportional over the tested range.

27
00:02:05.173 --> 00:02:09.133
Channels can saturate too, usually only at much higher concentrations.

28
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:14.133
Saturation points toward a carrier, but it is evidence, not proof.

29
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:20.047
Tracer experiments add a detectable tag to some substrate molecules.

30
00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:25.647
The counter sees tagged molecules entering, not the total amount inside.

31
00:02:26.987 --> 00:02:31.247
Take carrier vesicles with substrate already equal inside and outside.

32
00:02:32.647 --> 00:02:34.167
Add tagged substrate outside.

33
00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:40.647
Tagged molecules enter as untagged ones leave, so the total inside stays the same.

34
00:02:40.647 --> 00:02:46.547
A chemical assay of total substrate supplies what the tracer counter lacks.

35
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.960
That is exchange, not net uptake.

36
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.220
Label has its own concentration difference, so it can run in while total substrate

37
00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.180
stays level. A rising count alone never proves accumulation.

38
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.860
So measure the total, and combine exchange with rate, saturation, and single-channel

39
00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.180
steps instead of trusting one clue.

40
00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:15.043
The hill from chapter one tells you which way traffic can run.

41
00:03:16.343 --> 00:03:18.683
The pattern of that traffic reveals the route.

42
00:03:20.103 --> 00:03:21.403
Square steps point to a pore.

43
00:03:22.823 --> 00:03:25.183
Saturation and exchange point toward a carrier.

44
00:03:26.643 --> 00:03:29.663
Mechanism lives in the measurements, not the family name.
